---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const lastUpdated = new Date('2026-08-17');
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy Policy — Ayomiposi Sotomi"
  description="How this site handles your data — in short, it barely does."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl lg:max-w-4xl px-6 py-20">
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl font-medium text-text md:text-5xl">
      Privacy Policy
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      Last updated:{' '}
      {lastUpdated.toLocaleDateString('en-US', {
        month: 'long',
        day: 'numeric',
        year: 'numeric',
        timeZone: 'UTC',
      })}
    </p>

    <div class="prose prose-neutral mt-10 max-w-none">
      <p>
        This site is static — Markdown files rendered into HTML, with no
        server, no database, and no user accounts. That keeps what it can
        collect about you to almost nothing by construction, not just by
        policy.
      </p>

      <h2>Cookies</h2>
      <p>
        This site does not use cookies. No tracking cookies, no
        analytics cookies, no "essential" cookies used as a workaround. If
        that ever changes, this page will be updated first.
      </p>

      <h2>Analytics</h2>
      <p>
        This site uses{' '}
        <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/">Cloudflare Web
        Analytics</a> to count page views. It is cookieless, does not
        track you across sites, does not build a profile of you, and does
        not use any client-side identifier — it reports aggregate numbers
        (which pages were visited, roughly when, from what kind of
        browser), not who you are. This site never sees IP addresses or
        any per-visitor data from it.
      </p>

      <h2>Hosting</h2>
      <p>
        This site is hosted on Cloudflare Pages. Cloudflare may process
        standard connection metadata (like your IP address) as part of
        serving the site, the same way any web host does.
      </p>

      <h2>Newsletter</h2>
      <p>
        The newsletter signup form (in the footer and at the end of posts)
        submits your email address directly to{' '}
        <a href="https://buttondown.com">Buttondown</a>, the third-party
        service that sends the newsletter. This site's own code never
        receives, stores, or sees that address — there's no server here to
        store it on. Buttondown requires you to confirm your email before
        you're subscribed (double opt-in), and every email includes a
        one-click unsubscribe link. See{' '}
        <a href="https://buttondown.com/privacy">Buttondown's own privacy
        policy</a> for how they handle subscriber data.
      </p>

      <h2>Slow claps</h2>
      <p>
        Posts have a "slow clap" button instead of comments. Clap counts
        are anonymous totals stored on this site's own infrastructure (a
        Cloudflare Worker). To keep counts honest, a one-way hash of your
        IP address is kept for up to 24 hours and then expires — the
        address itself is never stored, and nothing ties a clap to you.
      </p>

      <h2>Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        This site itself collects no personal data — there's nothing here
        to request access to, correct, or delete. If you've subscribed to
        the newsletter, those rights (access, correction, deletion) apply
        to the data Buttondown holds on their end; unsubscribing removes
        you from future sends, and you can also contact Buttondown directly
        to request deletion of your subscriber record.
      </p>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about this policy, or a request about your data? Email{' '}
        <a href="mailto:blackjesus.root@proton.me">blackjesus.root@proton.me</a>
        {' '}— no account on any platform required.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
